[
  {
    "lesson_id": "067",
    "trigger_word": "perspective",
    "trigger_card_number": 832,
    "title": "LESSON 67: SIGNPOSTING",
    "header_color": "cyan",
    "pages": [
      {
        "page": 1,
        "total_pages": 2,
        "content": [
          "Formal writing tells the reader where it",
          "is going. These are the signposts.",
          "",
          "Adding: moreover, furthermore,",
          "in addition.",
          "Contrasting: however, nevertheless,",
          "whereas, conversely.",
          "Concluding: hence, therefore, thus,",
          "consequently."
        ]
      },
      {
        "page": 2,
        "total_pages": 2,
        "content": [
          "Framing a topic:",
          "in terms of = measured by.",
          "with regard to / as regards = about.",
          "from a ... perspective = seen one way.",
          "",
          "\"From a broader perspective, the",
          "decision was not merely rash; it was",
          "inevitable.\"",
          "",
          "Result with a purpose: thereby + -ing.",
          "\"cutting costs, thereby saving the site\"",
          "",
          "whereas joins two clauses inside ONE",
          "sentence; however joins two sentences.",
          "",
          "Many languages signpost with word order",
          "and particles; English writes the",
          "signpost out and puts a comma after it."
        ]
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "lesson_id": "068",
    "trigger_word": "classroom",
    "trigger_card_number": 901,
    "title": "LESSON 68: SO DO I",
    "header_color": "cyan",
    "pages": [
      {
        "page": 1,
        "total_pages": 2,
        "content": [
          "English hates repeating itself. It",
          "leaves the second half out, or swaps in",
          "a small word.",
          "",
          "Agreeing: so + helper + subject.",
          "\"I liked it.\" \"So did I.\"",
          "Agreeing with a negative:",
          "\"I don't smoke.\" \"Neither do I.\"",
          "",
          "Note the inverted order after so and",
          "neither."
        ]
      },
      {
        "page": 2,
        "total_pages": 2,
        "content": [
          "one / ones replaces a countable noun:",
          "\"Our classroom has no windows, and",
          "neither does the one next door.\"",
          "",
          "do so / do it replaces a whole verb",
          "phrase:",
          "\"She asked him to wait, and he did so.\"",
          "",
          "Or leave the words out entirely:",
          "\"Coming?\" \"If not, tell me.\" \"Why?\"",
          "",
          "Only leave out what the listener can",
          "put back. If in doubt, say it.",
          "",
          "Many languages simply drop the subject",
          "and the rest; English keeps the helper",
          "behind as a stand-in."
        ]
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "lesson_id": "069",
    "trigger_word": "how are you",
    "trigger_card_number": 976,
    "title": "LESSON 69: THE EXIT DOOR",
    "header_color": "cyan",
    "pages": [
      {
        "page": 1,
        "total_pages": 2,
        "content": [
          "One thing to say. Five ways to say it,",
          "from the doorstep to the report.",
          "",
          "The thing: you want to know how",
          "somebody is.",
          "",
          "1 Formula: \"How are you?\"",
          "2 Polite request: \"Would you mind",
          "telling me how she is?\"",
          "3 Neutral: \"I would like to know how",
          "she is.\""
        ]
      },
      {
        "page": 2,
        "total_pages": 2,
        "content": [
          "4 Hedged: \"She is thought to be",
          "recovering.\"",
          "5 Passive report: \"It is reported that",
          "her condition is unchanged.\"",
          "",
          "Same fact, five distances. Choosing well",
          "IS the skill: 1 in a report reads as",
          "careless, 5 on a doorstep as cold.",
          "",
          "And that is the end. You began the 3000",
          "naming what sits on a table. You leave",
          "holding the conditionals, the clefts,",
          "the subjunctive, the participle and the",
          "passive report — and the judgment to",
          "choose among them.",
          "",
          "The door is open. Go and read something",
          "difficult."
        ]
      }
    ]
  }
]
